it was one of the worst natural  disasters of ancient times  in the year 79 a.d mount vesuvius  erupted  burying the roman city of pompeii in 30  feet of ash and volcanic rock  excavations have been underway ever  since the 1700s  but almost 300 years later pompeii  is still giving up its secrets including  this incredible find revealed just this  week  it's an eatery the ancient equivalent of  a fast food restaurant  unearthed in a section of the city not  yet open to the public  known as thermapolium the latin word for  hot food counter these food shops  were quite common across the city but  this is the first time we've got to  study it with  all the tools that the 21st century  allows us to use the venue featured a  multi-sided food counter  complete with holes to store hot serving  pots and its facade  adorned with intriguing and colorful  frescoes including an illustration of  the shop itself  a trademark of sorts and what was served  may have been advertised in these vivid  images including  ducks and rooster while three quarters  of pompeii has already been excavated  there is still much to be discovered and  sometimes it's not the grand arenas and  marble temples that teach us the most  about the lives people led two millennia  ago  one of the things that we can learn from  this restaurant is that the ancient  roman world  was very much like our world today it is  an  urban world it's a world full of snack  shops  just like we have cafes on our corners  they had little snack shops in theirs  you could get hot food a drink a  conversation  and the understanding that 2000 years  later life would be something like it is  now  way back then what do you know  they ate and drank 2 000 years ago  cheers to that  why not
 

